  "text": "The creditor was not coming for her furniture. He was coming for her sons. Second Kings four, opening scene. A widow cries out to Elisha and lays out the situation. Her husband is dead. He feared the Lord. And the creditor is coming to take her two sons to be his slaves. Not the furniture. The children. That is the debt system she is trapped inside. And Elisha asks a question I think about a lot. \"What shall I do for thee? tell me, what hast thou in the house?\" And she says, \"Thine handmaid hath not any thing in the house, save a pot of oil.\" Nothing. Except one thing. And he says go and borrow vessels from all your neighbours. Empty ones. And then, this is the line, \"borrow not a few.\" So she does. Shuts the door on herself and her sons. And starts pouring. And it keeps coming. Vessel after vessel after vessel. Until she says to her son, \"Bring me yet a vessel.\" And he says, \"There is not a vessel more.\" And the oil stayed. Read that again. The supply stopped exactly when the containers did. She sells it. Pays the debt. Keeps her sons. And lives on what is left.",
